1
00:00:02,150 --> 00:00:06,444
Hello friends, and welcome to the channel.

2
00:00:06,694 --> 00:00:17,729
Today we'll build a complete, professional Sales MIS Report
— exactly the way MIS executives build it in companies.

3
00:00:17,979 --> 00:00:29,185
MIS means Management Information System — summarising
raw data so management can make a decision at a glance.

4
00:00:29,435 --> 00:00:39,915
By the end of this video you'll have a live dashboard — KPIs,
region and product analysis, Pivot Tables, charts and slicers.

5
00:00:40,165 --> 00:00:46,401
I won't just show it — every formula
is explained too. Let's get started!

6
00:00:46,651 --> 00:00:56,642
First, let's understand the data. This is the Raw_Data
sheet — it holds 180 transactions for October 2026.

7
00:00:56,892 --> 00:01:05,090
Date is the invoice date, Invoice No is each bill's
unique number, and Region is East, West, North or South.

8
00:01:05,340 --> 00:01:13,047
Then Salesperson, Product — Tea A, Tea B
or Tea C, Qty (units sold) and Unit Price.

9
00:01:13,297 --> 00:01:22,072
Sales Amount is empty for now — we'll fill it with
a formula. Payment Status shows Paid or Pending.

10
00:01:22,322 --> 00:01:35,408
Shortcut: Ctrl + ↓ — the cursor jumps straight to the last
row, 182. So there are 180 data rows below the header.

11
00:01:35,658 --> 00:01:46,182
Important: one row = one unique invoice. Every
Invoice No is filled in and never repeats.

12
00:01:46,432 --> 00:01:54,971
This assumption is written in the Read_Me
sheet — Total Orders will be based on it later.

13
00:01:55,221 --> 00:02:06,620
Now let's convert this data into an Excel Table — formulas
become readable and the range expands automatically.

14
00:02:06,870 --> 00:02:11,953
Click any cell in the data and press Ctrl + T.

15
00:02:12,203 --> 00:02:23,433
Create Table picks up the range =$A$2:$I$182.
'My table has headers' must be ticked — then OK.

16
00:02:23,683 --> 00:02:33,461
The table is ready — banded rows, a filter button on
every header, and a Table Design tab in the ribbon.

17
00:02:33,711 --> 00:02:42,166
In the Table Name box type SalesData and press
Enter. Every formula from here on uses this name.

18
00:02:42,416 --> 00:02:52,471
Now Sales Amount. Inside a table, formulas use structured
references — the column name, not the cell address.

19
00:02:52,721 --> 00:03:03,735
In H3 type: =[@Qty]*[@[Unit Price]]

20
00:03:03,985 --> 00:03:13,507
@ means the value from this same row. Unit
Price is two words, so it needs double brackets.

21
00:03:13,757 --> 00:03:22,275
Press Enter and the formula fills the whole
column automatically — all 180 rows at once.

22
00:03:22,525 --> 00:03:36,441
Check: row 3 is 55 × 120.00 = 6,600.00 ✔. Select the
column and the status bar shows Sum: 1,428,190.00.

23
00:03:36,691 --> 00:03:45,338
Now the heart of the dashboard — KPI cards.
Remember: never type final values by hand.

24
00:03:45,588 --> 00:03:52,292
Everything comes from formulas — when the data
changes, the numbers update by themselves.

25
00:03:52,542 --> 00:04:08,486
Total Sales (B6): SUMIFS adds only October's
Sales Amount — the date range comes from DATE().

26
00:04:08,736 --> 00:04:13,712
Result: 1,428,190.00 — that's 14 lakh 28 thousand 190.

27
00:04:13,962 --> 00:04:25,021
Total Orders (D6): one row = one invoice, so
COUNTIFS counts October's non-blank invoices — 180.

28
00:04:25,271 --> 00:04:34,794
Total Units Sold (F6): same date
condition, SUMIFS on Qty — 11,834.

29
00:04:35,044 --> 00:04:50,626
Average Sales / Order (H6): =IFERROR(B6/D6,0) — no
error even if orders are zero. Result 7,934.39.

30
00:04:50,876 --> 00:05:03,962
Pending Sales Amount (B9): SUMIFS where
Payment Status = "Pending" — 372,985.00.

31
00:05:04,212 --> 00:05:17,211
It's deliberately called Pending Sales Amount, not 'Outstanding
Receivables' — the data has no payment-received or balance field.

32
00:05:17,461 --> 00:05:24,999
Now region-wise analysis — management
wants to see which region is ahead.

33
00:05:25,249 --> 00:05:37,821
C13 has the same SUMIFS plus one condition: SalesData[Region] =
B13. The region comes from a cell, so the formula copies down.

34
00:05:38,071 --> 00:05:56,662
East 351,030 • West 383,675 (strongest)
• North 355,130 • South 338,355.

35
00:05:56,912 --> 00:06:07,095
Total (C17) = 1,428,190.00 — exactly equal
to Total Sales. That's our cross-check ✔

36
00:06:07,345 --> 00:06:18,979
Same for Product-wise: Tea A 385,320 • Tea B 432,820
• Tea C 610,050 — the total is again 1,428,190.00 ✔

37
00:06:19,229 --> 00:06:25,891
Along with formulas, let's learn Pivot Tables
— an instant summary with drag-and-drop.

38
00:06:26,141 --> 00:06:33,891
Click any cell in SalesData → Insert
→ PivotTable → From Table/Range.

39
00:06:34,141 --> 00:06:41,551
Table/Range already shows SalesData.
Choose New Worksheet and click OK.

40
00:06:41,801 --> 00:06:53,349
A new sheet appears with PivotTable Fields on the
right. Drag Region to Rows and Sales Amount to Values.

41
00:06:53,599 --> 00:07:04,593
Note: Values should say Sum of Sales Amount. If
you see 'Count of' → Value Field Settings → Sum.

42
00:07:04,843 --> 00:07:16,797
Put Date in Filters and keep October 2026.
Grand Total = 1428190 — matches the dashboard ✔

43
00:07:17,047 --> 00:07:24,157
Build a second pivot the same way —
Product in Rows, Sales Amount in Values.

44
00:07:24,407 --> 00:07:30,898
Now charts — visuals make the
point to management instantly.

45
00:07:31,148 --> 00:07:39,516
Select the Region pivot → PivotTable Analyze
→ PivotChart → Clustered Column → OK.

46
00:07:39,766 --> 00:07:51,614
The chart is in. Right-click → Add Data Labels — the exact
value on every bar. Title: Region-wise Sales Performance.

47
00:07:51,864 --> 00:08:02,048
For the Product pivot, a Pie chart — Product-wise
Sales Analysis. Tea C has the biggest share.

48
00:08:02,298 --> 00:08:07,402
Now let's make the report interactive — with slicers.

49
00:08:07,652 --> 00:08:15,679
Select a pivot → PivotTable Analyze → Insert
Slicer → tick Region and Product → OK.

50
00:08:15,929 --> 00:08:27,327
One slicer for both pivots: right-click the slicer
→ Report Connections → tick both PivotTables.

51
00:08:27,577 --> 00:08:35,668
Remember: a slicer can only connect pivots
built from the same data source (SalesData).

52
00:08:35,918 --> 00:08:42,964
Now click West — both pivots and both
charts filter to West instantly!

53
00:08:43,214 --> 00:08:50,601
To remove the filter, use the slicer's Clear
Filter button — the totals are back in full.

54
00:08:50,851 --> 00:08:59,135
Now the finishing — a professional
dashboard means clarity, not decoration.

55
00:08:59,385 --> 00:09:09,311
Bold title, consistent colour for the KPI cards, and
a thousands separator on numbers — Ctrl + Shift + 1.

56
00:09:09,561 --> 00:09:15,881
View tab → untick Gridlines —
the look becomes perfectly clean.

57
00:09:16,131 --> 00:09:24,757
One font, consistent spacing, two or three colours —
the report is for a manager, so readability comes first.

58
00:09:25,007 --> 00:09:32,735
A report isn't trustworthy until
it's refreshed and validated.

59
00:09:32,985 --> 00:09:42,143
When a new row lands in Raw_Data, the Table
makes SUMIFS / COUNTIFS pick it up automatically.

60
00:09:42,393 --> 00:09:51,849
Pivot Tables must be refreshed: Data
→ Refresh All — or Ctrl + Alt + F5.

61
00:09:52,099 --> 00:10:02,153
Three cross-checks: Region total = Product total
= Pivot Grand Total = Total Sales 1,428,190.

62
00:10:02,403 --> 00:10:08,105
All three match ✔ — the report is reconciled.

63
00:10:08,355 --> 00:10:14,355
So friends, today we built a complete
Sales MIS Report from scratch.

64
00:10:14,605 --> 00:10:25,597
Excel Table, structured formula, SUMIFS / COUNTIFS KPIs,
region and product analysis, Pivot, charts and slicers.

65
00:10:25,847 --> 00:10:35,305
And most important — we validated every number.
That's what makes you a professional MIS executive.

66
00:10:35,555 --> 00:10:48,191
Enjoyed the video? Like, Subscribe, and tell us
your next topic in the comments. Happy Excel-ing!
